<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Renaissance</h1><p class="path-tagline">Florence, the Medici and the recovery of the ancient world: how a revival of learning remade European art and thought.</p><p class="path-intro">From the fourteenth century Italian scholars set out to recover the learning of Greece and Rome, hunting for lost manuscripts and learning Greek from Byzantine teachers. In Florence, a republic of bankers and wool merchants, that revival met money and ambition: patrons paid for new churches, domes, sculpture and painting, and artists studied perspective and the human body as never before. This path follows the Renaissance from Petrarch to the Sistine ceiling, Machiavelli's realism and Erasmus's Greek New Testament, as it spread from Italy across Europe.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrarch.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1304–1374</span><span class="path-step-name">Petrarch</span><span class="path-step-why">Poet of the Canzoniere, whose search for classical manuscripts made him a founder of Renaissance humanism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/republic-of-florence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1115 CE – 1532 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Republic of Florence</span><span class="path-step-why">A republic of bankers and wool merchants whose gold florin became a standard coin of European trade, and the birthplace of the Renaissance.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1350–1415</span><span class="path-step-name">Manuel Chrysoloras</span><span class="path-step-why">From 1397 this Byzantine scholar taught Greek in Florence, opening classical Greek literature to the Italian humanists.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1377–1446</span><span class="path-step-name">Filippo Brunelleschi</span><span class="path-step-why">His dome for Florence Cathedral, built without wooden centring, is still the largest masonry dome in the world; he is also credited with the first demonstration of linear perspective.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1449–1492</span><span class="path-step-name">Lorenzo de' Medici</span><span class="path-step-why">Florence's effective ruler from 1469: poet, diplomat and patron of Botticelli and the young Michelangelo.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1452–1519</span><span class="path-step-name">Leonardo da Vinci</span><span class="path-step-why">Painter of the Last Supper and the Mona Lisa, whose notebooks studied anatomy, mechanics and nature.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1473–1481</span><span class="path-step-name">Sistine Chapel</span><span class="path-step-why">Botticelli and Perugino painted its walls; Michelangelo painted its ceiling between 1508 and 1512 and, later, the Last Judgement.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-prince.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1513</span><span class="path-step-name">The Prince</span><span class="path-step-why">Machiavelli's treatise of 1513 on how new princes win and hold power, written after the Medici's return had cost him his office.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erasmus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1466–1536</span><span class="path-step-name">Erasmus</span><span class="path-step-why">The most famous scholar of the Northern Renaissance, whose Greek New Testament of 1516 and Praise of Folly made him a voice for reform.</span></span></a></li></ol>
